1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,330
In 2016, China launched a satellite
called Micius.

2
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:08,460
One of its jobs was to make pairs
of photons and split them up.

3
00:00:08,994 --> 00:00:12,874
One photon of each pair went down
to an observatory in one city,

4
00:00:13,194 --> 00:00:17,254
the other to an observatory in another,
twelve hundred kilometres away.

5
00:00:17,986 --> 00:00:21,186
At each end, a detector measured
the photon that arrived.

6
00:00:21,266 --> 00:00:25,746
And the results were linked more tightly
than anything ordinary allows.

7
00:00:26,575 --> 00:00:28,575
Is something passing between them?

8
00:00:28,835 --> 00:00:32,915
In 2008, physicists near Geneva
tried to time it,

9
00:00:33,145 --> 00:00:36,375
between two villages
eighteen kilometres apart.

10
00:00:36,719 --> 00:00:39,259
Assuming the universe has
a preferred frame,

11
00:00:39,479 --> 00:00:42,219
in which Earth moves slowly
compared to light,

12
00:00:42,399 --> 00:00:45,339
they found that anything travelling
from one photon to the other

13
00:00:45,569 --> 00:00:49,419
would need to go at least
ten thousand times faster than light.

14
00:00:50,063 --> 00:00:54,143
That's a limit on something hypothetical,
not a speed anyone measured.

15
00:00:54,863 --> 00:00:56,383
But it's a strange one.

16
00:00:57,124 --> 00:00:59,244
So here's the obvious idea.

17
00:00:59,924 --> 00:01:03,584
Keep one photon of each pair,
and give the other to a friend on Mars.

18
00:01:04,147 --> 00:01:07,687
Today a radio message to Mars
takes up to twenty-two minutes.

19
00:01:08,527 --> 00:01:12,327
With entangled photons,
you measure yours, and your friend knows.

20
00:01:13,077 --> 00:01:14,297
It doesn't work.

21
00:01:14,397 --> 00:01:17,477
Not with better detectors,
not with better satellites.

22
00:01:17,557 --> 00:01:20,257
Not ever, as far as physics knows.

23
00:01:20,807 --> 00:01:23,367
And the reason is stranger
than the link itself.

24
00:01:26,117 --> 00:01:28,637
First, what entanglement actually is.

25
00:01:29,377 --> 00:01:33,917
A source makes two particles together
and sends one to Alice, and one to Bob.

26
00:01:34,535 --> 00:01:37,815
Each of them measures a particle
and gets one of two answers.

27
00:01:38,615 --> 00:01:40,255
Call them zero and one.

28
00:01:40,862 --> 00:01:42,642
Alice's answer is random.

29
00:01:42,902 --> 00:01:44,242
So is Bob's.

30
00:01:44,322 --> 00:01:48,222
But when they set their detectors
the same way, the answers match.

31
00:01:49,022 --> 00:01:51,102
In the ideal case, every time.

32
00:01:51,673 --> 00:01:55,833
Einstein never accepted that quantum
mechanics was the complete story.

33
00:01:56,513 --> 00:02:01,733
In a letter to Max Born in 1947, he called
this spooky action at a distance.

34
00:02:03,463 --> 00:02:06,103
But hold on. Is that really so strange?

35
00:02:06,887 --> 00:02:10,147
The physicist John Bell
had a colleague, Reinhold Bertlmann,

36
00:02:10,227 --> 00:02:12,927
who liked to wear two socks
of different colours.

37
00:02:13,597 --> 00:02:17,057
See that one sock is pink,
and you know the other one isn't.

38
00:02:17,497 --> 00:02:19,297
Instantly, at any distance.

39
00:02:19,397 --> 00:02:21,797
Nothing spooky about it.

40
00:02:22,337 --> 00:02:24,457
So maybe particles are the same.

41
00:02:25,157 --> 00:02:29,417
Maybe they carry their answers from the
start, like socks chosen that morning.

42
00:02:30,008 --> 00:02:34,968
In 1981, Bell used those socks
to explain why that idea fails.

43
00:02:36,272 --> 00:02:40,692
Here's the simplest version of the test,
from the physicist David Mermin.

44
00:02:40,932 --> 00:02:44,812
Two boxes, far apart,
each with a switch with three positions.

45
00:02:45,732 --> 00:02:49,292
A particle arrives,
and the box flashes red or green.

46
00:02:49,697 --> 00:02:52,517
For every particle,
Alice and Bob each pick a position

47
00:02:52,747 --> 00:02:55,677
at random,
without knowing what the other picked.

48
00:02:55,985 --> 00:02:59,845
When they happen to pick the same
position, the colours always match.

49
00:03:00,604 --> 00:03:03,924
So suppose each pair of particles
carries instructions.

50
00:03:04,424 --> 00:03:07,924
A colour for each position,
the same list in both.

51
00:03:08,595 --> 00:03:10,635
Take the list red, red, green.

52
00:03:10,975 --> 00:03:13,535
There are nine ways
to set the two switches.

53
00:03:14,235 --> 00:03:16,195
In five of them, the colours match.

54
00:03:16,751 --> 00:03:20,551
Try any other list.
None gives fewer than five.

55
00:03:21,011 --> 00:03:23,071
So if the answers are written in advance,

56
00:03:23,151 --> 00:03:25,871
the colours match
at least five times out of nine.

57
00:03:26,494 --> 00:03:29,074
Quantum mechanics predicts exactly half.

58
00:03:31,234 --> 00:03:33,474
Five ninths against one half.

59
00:03:34,134 --> 00:03:37,794
Experiments have tested
gaps like this since 1972,

60
00:03:38,014 --> 00:03:39,994
and they side with quantum mechanics.

61
00:03:40,825 --> 00:03:45,105
In 2015, a team in Delft
closed the two biggest loopholes at once.

62
00:03:45,185 --> 00:03:50,438
And in 2022, three pioneers
of these experiments, John Clauser,

63
00:03:50,668 --> 00:03:56,125
Alain Aspect and Anton Zeilinger,
shared the Nobel Prize for their own work.

64
00:03:56,689 --> 00:03:59,369
So the answers can't all be written
in advance,

65
00:03:59,689 --> 00:04:02,049
at least not in any way that stays local.

66
00:04:02,373 --> 00:04:05,733
That doesn't mean one particle
sends a signal to the other.

67
00:04:06,253 --> 00:04:09,433
And that is exactly where
our message runs into trouble.

68
00:04:11,983 --> 00:04:13,603
Let's try to send one.

69
00:04:14,303 --> 00:04:16,523
Alice wants to send Bob a single bit.

70
00:04:16,983 --> 00:04:19,783
She measures her particle
and gets whatever she gets.

71
00:04:20,467 --> 00:04:24,567
She can't choose.
Zero or one, the result is random.

72
00:04:25,387 --> 00:04:29,107
Bob gets the matching answer,
but Alice didn't put anything in it.

73
00:04:29,355 --> 00:04:32,635
And Bob can't even tell
whether Alice measured at all.

74
00:04:33,227 --> 00:04:35,687
Say Bob measures a thousand particles.

75
00:04:35,767 --> 00:04:40,001
About five hundred zeros,
about five hundred ones.

76
00:04:40,301 --> 00:04:43,381
Now Alice measures her side,
any way she likes.

77
00:04:44,001 --> 00:04:45,801
Bob measures another thousand.

78
00:04:46,421 --> 00:04:48,976
About five hundred, and five hundred.

79
00:04:49,075 --> 00:04:50,975
Nothing on his side changes.

80
00:04:51,948 --> 00:04:56,188
The connection only becomes visible
when Alice and Bob compare their records.

81
00:04:56,288 --> 00:05:00,628
And to compare them, they have to send
them, at the speed of light or slower.

82
00:05:03,348 --> 00:05:05,408
But Alice does control one thing.

83
00:05:05,928 --> 00:05:07,828
She chooses how to set her detector.

84
00:05:08,808 --> 00:05:11,088
In Mermin's boxes, that's the switch.

85
00:05:11,728 --> 00:05:15,168
Her choice changes how her results
line up with Bob's.

86
00:05:15,398 --> 00:05:17,668
But that only shows when they compare.

87
00:05:18,271 --> 00:05:20,091
So could Bob notice it?

88
00:05:20,651 --> 00:05:23,771
Position one means zero,
position two means one?

89
00:05:24,321 --> 00:05:26,521
No. Whatever Alice does to her particle,

90
00:05:26,601 --> 00:05:29,281
measuring it, turning it,
or leaving it alone,

91
00:05:29,561 --> 00:05:32,961
Bob still sees red half the time
and green half the time.

92
00:05:33,505 --> 00:05:38,545
It's called the no-communication theorem,
and physicists proved it around 1980.

93
00:05:39,361 --> 00:05:44,701
Now imagine Alice could force her result,
and the link still held. Force a zero.

94
00:05:45,041 --> 00:05:46,163
Force a one.

95
00:05:46,243 --> 00:05:48,923
Then Bob would get her bit
every time he measured.

96
00:05:49,603 --> 00:05:52,063
That would be a message,
faster than light.

97
00:05:52,962 --> 00:05:55,562
So the randomness isn't a flaw
in our equipment.

98
00:05:55,942 --> 00:05:59,242
It's part of the reason
the link can't carry a message.

99
00:06:01,822 --> 00:06:03,982
But that's standard quantum mechanics.

100
00:06:04,282 --> 00:06:07,582
What if the theory itself
is very slightly wrong?

101
00:06:08,213 --> 00:06:12,093
In 1989, Steven Weinberg,
who had won a Nobel Prize

102
00:06:12,323 --> 00:06:15,373
ten years earlier, asked exactly that.

103
00:06:15,947 --> 00:06:19,827
Weinberg wrote down a version
with a tiny change to its mathematics,

104
00:06:20,127 --> 00:06:22,507
a so-called nonlinear correction.

105
00:06:23,367 --> 00:06:25,167
He wasn't claiming it was correct.

106
00:06:25,667 --> 00:06:28,027
He wanted experiments to test it.

107
00:06:28,569 --> 00:06:32,509
Experiments looked for it
with extreme precision, and found nothing.

108
00:06:33,124 --> 00:06:34,864
Then came a deeper problem.

109
00:06:35,684 --> 00:06:39,004
In 1990, Nicolas Gisin showed
that Weinberg's theory,

110
00:06:39,234 --> 00:06:43,104
with the usual rules of measurement,
would let Alice signal Bob.

111
00:06:43,361 --> 00:06:45,301
Instantly. At any distance.

112
00:06:46,566 --> 00:06:48,866
Joseph Polchinski looked closer.

113
00:06:49,526 --> 00:06:52,166
You could block the signal
between Alice and Bob,

114
00:06:52,246 --> 00:06:56,546
but the theory still allowed communication
between branches of the wave function.

115
00:06:56,881 --> 00:07:00,861
In principle, between branches
where an observer got different outcomes.

116
00:07:01,681 --> 00:07:03,501
He called it the Everett phone.

117
00:07:04,126 --> 00:07:07,066
Not every nonlinear theory
has to fail this way.

118
00:07:07,586 --> 00:07:09,086
But the lesson was clear.

119
00:07:09,646 --> 00:07:12,666
The rule against signalling
isn't an extra restriction.

120
00:07:13,166 --> 00:07:15,526
It follows from standard
quantum mechanics.

121
00:07:18,156 --> 00:07:19,556
Why does that matter?

122
00:07:20,276 --> 00:07:22,896
As our video on faster-than-light travel
showed,

123
00:07:23,016 --> 00:07:26,256
a signal faster than light
could arrive before it was sent.

124
00:07:26,937 --> 00:07:30,057
Entanglement doesn't let information
cross that line.

125
00:07:30,737 --> 00:07:33,737
It creates correlations,
but carries no signal.

126
00:07:34,373 --> 00:07:37,933
Even quantum teleportation,
from our video on teleportation,

127
00:07:38,193 --> 00:07:41,733
needs two ordinary bits,
sent the ordinary way.

128
00:07:42,041 --> 00:07:45,541
Without them, Bob's result
is indistinguishable from noise.

129
00:07:48,031 --> 00:07:50,091
So that's the first mystery solved.

130
00:07:50,791 --> 00:07:52,991
Entanglement can't carry a message.

131
00:07:53,311 --> 00:07:54,991
But there's a second one.

132
00:07:55,411 --> 00:07:59,231
The strength of the link can be measured,
and it has a surprising limit.

133
00:08:01,791 --> 00:08:02,991
Here's a game.

134
00:08:03,491 --> 00:08:08,711
Alice and Bob each get a random question,
zero or one, and each answers zero or one.

135
00:08:09,228 --> 00:08:10,468
They can't talk.

136
00:08:10,788 --> 00:08:14,528
They win if their answers match,
except when both get question one.

137
00:08:15,148 --> 00:08:16,948
Then the answers must differ.

138
00:08:17,627 --> 00:08:19,487
With any plan agreed in advance,

139
00:08:19,567 --> 00:08:22,867
the best they can do
is win three times out of four.

140
00:08:23,397 --> 00:08:27,017
With entangled particles,
they can win about eighty-five per cent

141
00:08:27,097 --> 00:08:30,977
of the time, and experiments
keep beating three in four.

142
00:08:31,172 --> 00:08:32,452
But not by more.

143
00:08:33,192 --> 00:08:36,172
In 1980, Boris Tsirelson proved
that quantum mechanics

144
00:08:36,402 --> 00:08:38,812
tops out at eighty-five point four.

145
00:08:39,977 --> 00:08:44,977
In 1994, Sandu Popescu and Daniel Rohrlich
asked a simple question.

146
00:08:45,737 --> 00:08:46,997
Why stop there?

147
00:08:47,499 --> 00:08:50,599
They imagined a box
that wins the game every time.

148
00:08:51,319 --> 00:08:53,739
It isn't a real device, just mathematics.

149
00:08:54,701 --> 00:08:56,241
But here's the surprise.

150
00:08:57,175 --> 00:08:59,035
It still can't send a message.

151
00:08:59,715 --> 00:09:02,815
Each side, on its own,
gives zero or one at random.

152
00:09:03,415 --> 00:09:06,155
Bob sees fifty-fifty, whatever Alice asks.

153
00:09:06,939 --> 00:09:10,939
Stronger links than nature allows,
and still nothing faster than light.

154
00:09:13,599 --> 00:09:16,209
So why does nature stop at eighty-five?

155
00:09:16,439 --> 00:09:19,093
Not seventy-five. Not a hundred.

156
00:09:19,993 --> 00:09:21,933
Physicists have proposed principles.

157
00:09:22,953 --> 00:09:25,793
One result shows that above about
ninety-one per cent,

158
00:09:25,873 --> 00:09:29,673
problems that normally need
enormous amounts of communication

159
00:09:29,833 --> 00:09:32,193
could be solved with just one bit.

160
00:09:32,484 --> 00:09:34,364
Another, called information causality,

161
00:09:34,594 --> 00:09:37,624
gives the same eighty-five
point four limit.

162
00:09:37,704 --> 00:09:42,464
But it doesn't fully explain why
quantum mechanics works the way it does.

163
00:09:42,878 --> 00:09:46,378
So far, no agreed principle explains
why nature allows exactly

164
00:09:46,608 --> 00:09:49,618
these correlations, and nothing stronger.

165
00:09:52,898 --> 00:09:55,418
So, two photons,
twelve hundred kilometres apart.

166
00:09:55,648 --> 00:09:57,698
Not one bit gets through.

167
00:09:57,928 --> 00:10:03,514
And no quantum link, however perfect,
gets past eighty-five per cent.

168
00:10:04,614 --> 00:10:07,194
We know why the link
can't carry a message.

169
00:10:07,674 --> 00:10:09,894
We still don't know why it isn't stronger.

170
00:10:11,704 --> 00:10:13,604
So here's a question for you.

171
00:10:14,264 --> 00:10:16,804
Is there a deeper rule
behind eighty-five per cent,

172
00:10:16,884 --> 00:10:19,824
or is that just how
our universe turned out?

173
00:10:20,144 --> 00:10:21,694
Let me know in the comments.
